# JARVIS — example environment file
# ===================================================================
# EVERYTHING HERE IS OPTIONAL in the default (bridge) mode. JARVIS runs
# perfectly with an empty file — the bridge authenticates off your Claude Code
# login, and voice input and output both fall back to the browser's own speech.
# Copy this to .env.local, and add only what you actually want to change.
#
# The one line worth adding is ELEVENLABS_API_KEY, down in the BRIDGE section:
# it gives you a much better voice and Scribe transcription. The free tier is
# plenty for a demo. Never commit a real key.
#
# Two audiences read these variables:
#   - the BRIDGE (the Node server, bridge/server.mjs) reads the JARVIS_* and
#     ELEVENLABS_API_KEY variables from its shell environment.
#   - the FRONTEND (the Vite app) reads the VITE_* variables from .env.local.
# Only VITE_-prefixed values ever reach the browser.


# ===================================================================
# BRIDGE (the server) — read from the shell that runs `npm run bridge`
# ===================================================================
# These are shown with their defaults. Leave them unset to accept the default.

# The language Claude answers in: ru (default) or en. If unset, the bridge also
# reads VITE_LANG from the shell, so `$env:VITE_LANG='en'` switches both halves.
# JARVIS_LANG=ru

# Port the bridge listens on (WebSocket + a few HTTP endpoints). Default 8787.
# If you change it, change VITE_BRIDGE_URL to match.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT=8787

# The model the bridge asks Claude Code to run. Default claude-opus-5.
# JARVIS_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Reasoning effort. Default medium.
# JARVIS_EFFORT=medium

# Allow effectful tools (send, tap, delete, install, pay). Off by default, so
# JARVIS can look at anything and change nothing. `npm run bridge:writes` sets
# this to 1 for you — read the decideTool gate in bridge/server.mjs first.
# It gives the agent a shell and the file tools with this user's rights, so a
# bridge running as root ignores it and stays read-only. Not isolation: as any
# user the shell reaches whatever that user can, keys included.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=0

# Allow JARVIS to create, move and delete Google Calendar events. Off by default:
# without it the calendar can be read but not changed. Narrower than
# JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES — it opens the calendar only, not the shell or the files —
# and the only switch that does: JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=1 does not open the calendar.
# Every change is read back and waits for its own spoken command: «записывай»,
# «переноси», «меняй» or «удаляй» (a plain «да» is not enough). Set it once
# for your Windows account with `setx JARVIS_CALENDAR_WRITES 1`, then restart.
# Connect the calendar itself first: `npm run calendar:connect -- <client.json>`.
# JARVIS_CALENDAR_WRITES=0

# The time zone calendar dates are read in. Default: the Google Calendar's own
# zone (learnt on the first request), else this computer's. Example: Europe/Moscow.
# JARVIS_TIMEZONE=

# Extra browser origins allowed to open the agent WebSocket, comma separated.
# Local dev origins are already allowed; add others only if you serve the app
# from a different address. Default: none.
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Allow clients that send no Origin header at all (curl, scripts, native apps).
# Off by default, because that is also what local malware looks like. Default 0.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_NO_ORIGIN=0

# The address the bridge listens on. Default 127.0.0.1 — loopback only; reach a
# server through an SSH tunnel. 0.0.0.0 puts the agent socket and the /fetch
# proxy on every interface: don't, unless a firewall stands in front.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Host names the bridge answers to besides localhost, comma separated — the
# guard against DNS rebinding. Hosts named in JARVIS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS count too.
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Extra folders the agent's pc_* tools may list, find and read in, separated
# by ";". The project is always one. Never / or /proc: on Linux that reaches
# /proc/self/environ, where the bridge's keys are. Default: none.
# JARVIS_PC_ROOTS=

# Extra directories the bridge's /file endpoint may serve from, comma separated.
# Your home directory and the system temp directory are always included; this is
# for anything outside them, such as an external drive of footage. Default: none.
# JARVIS_FILE_ROOTS=

# A second Claude login, for when the first one's usage limit is reached. Off
# by default. Sign the second account in to its own directory, on this machine,
# with Claude Code's own login — never copy a credentials file:
#   CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=/path/to/dir claude auth login
# then name that directory here. When the primary login hits its limit, a
# question that had not yet called a tool or said a word is asked once more on
# the backup, in a new session (without the conversation so far); the primary
# is used again from the first turn after its limit lifts. Logs name the logins
# "primary" and "backup" only. Whether running two subscriptions this way fits
# your plan's terms is yours to check before you turn it on.
# JARVIS_CLAUDE_BACKUP_DIR=

# Fallback channel for when the Claude usage limit is reached. Off by default,
# and on only when every line below is set. It is the Claude API on its own API
# key, billed per request — never another Claude subscription, and a login token
# (sk-ant-oat…) is refused. Text only: no tools, so it cannot search, show,
# change or repeat anything. A turn that had already called a tool when the
# limit hit is not sent to it. Spend stops at the daily budget (in dollars,
# kept in ~/.jarvis/fallback-spend.json across restarts).
# JARVIS_FALLBACK=anthropic-api
# JARVIS_FALLBACK_API_KEY=
# JARVIS_FALLBACK_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# JARVIS_FALLBACK_BUDGET_USD=1
# Only for a model the bridge has no price for: $ per million tokens.
# JARVIS_FALLBACK_PRICE_IN=
# JARVIS_FALLBACK_PRICE_OUT=
# Per answer, per request (no retries), and an optional effort level.
# JARVIS_FALLBACK_MAX_TOKENS=2000
# JARVIS_FALLBACK_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# JARVIS_FALLBACK_EFFORT=

# ElevenLabs voice id for the bridge's own speech endpoint. Default: George.
# JARVIS_VOICE_ID=JBFqnCBsd6RMkjVDRZzb

# ElevenLabs API key. Optional. With it, JARVIS speaks in the ElevenLabs voice
# and transcribes with ElevenLabs Scribe; without it, everything still works on
# the browser's own speech. If you already have an elevenlabs MCP server in
# ~/.claude.json, the bridge reads the key from there and you can leave this
# blank. Default: none.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# Deepgram API key, for streaming recognition through the bridge. Optional; it
# stays on the bridge — the page gets the transcript, never the key. Default: none.
# DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=

# Which engine does realtime recognition: deepgram or elevenlabs. Unset, a
# Deepgram key wins over an ElevenLabs one; pinned to one without its key, the
# browser's own recognition is used.
# JARVIS_STT=

# The local knowledge base (answers you taught JARVIS). JARVIS_KB=0 turns it
# off; JARVIS_KB_DIR moves it from the project's data/ folder. Personal data:
# never committed. Manage it with `npm run kb`.
# JARVIS_KB=1
# JARVIS_KB_DIR=

# With VITE_TRACE=1, page trace steps go to logs/trace.jsonl. The words of the
# conversation stay out of that log unless this is 1 (and VITE_DIALOG_LOG=1 in
# .env.local for the page's side). Default 0.
# JARVIS_DIALOG_LOG=0

# None of the bridge's keys or JARVIS_* settings reach the agent: Claude Code
# gets the system's basics, the proxy settings and its own ANTHROPIC_* /
# CLAUDE_* configuration only (bridge/secrets.mjs).


# ===================================================================
# FRONTEND (Vite) — put these in .env.local
# ===================================================================

# Which brain the browser talks to. Default bridge.
#   bridge — talk to the local bridge (recommended). No API key, and every MCP
#            server in your Claude Code config is available.
#   direct — the browser calls the Claude API itself. Needs VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.
# VITE_BACKEND=bridge

# Language of recognition, voice and filler lines. Default ru.
#   ru — ru-RU recognition in Chrome/Edge and a Russian system voice.
#   en — the original English JARVIS.
# The wake word answers to both "Джарвис" and "Jarvis" either way.
# VITE_LANG=ru

# Where the bridge listens. Keep the port in step with JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT above.
# Default ws://localhost:8787.
# VITE_BRIDGE_URL=ws://localhost:8787

# Browser text-to-speech engine, used when no ElevenLabs voice is active.
# Default system.
#   system — the browser's built-in speechSynthesis. Instant, on-device.
#   kokoro — a neural voice running in the browser. Better sound, slower to start.
# VITE_TTS_ENGINE=system

# Which Kokoro voice, when VITE_TTS_ENGINE=kokoro. Default bm_george.
# VITE_KOKORO_VOICE=bm_george

# Prefer the ElevenLabs voice from the browser when a key is available.
# Default false.
# VITE_USE_ELEVENLABS=false

# Anthropic API key — DIRECT MODE ONLY. Required if and only if
# VITE_BACKEND=direct, and it ships to the browser, so use it only for a local
# demo. The default bridge mode never reads it. Default: none.
# VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
